## 3. Propulsion Dynamics & Mass-Power-Time Trilemma

### 3.1 NEP Thrust Physics & Realistic Mass Scaling

The Tsiolkovsky rocket equation governs interplanetary transfer: $\Delta v = I_{sp} g_0 \ln(m_0/m_f)$. For Mars fast-transfer trajectories, $\Delta v \approx 6$ km/s/leg.

Jet power for electric propulsion: $P_j = F v_e / 2$, with $F = 2 \eta P_{\text{in}} / v_e$. For $I_{sp} = 5000$ s ($v_e = 49$ km/s), $\eta = 0.6$:

$$F = 2.45 \times 10^{-5} \cdot P_{\text{in}} \text{ [N/W]}$$

For $t_{\text{transit}} = 120$ d = $1.04 \times 10^7$ s: $a_{\text{req}} \approx 5.77 \times 10^{-4}$ m/s² → $F_{\text{req}} \approx 57.7$ N → $P_{\text{in, req}} \approx 2.36$ MWe.

### 3.2 Updated TRL Assessment (2025–2026)

**Critical revision:** Prior analyses placed NEP at TRL 4. The NASA Mars Transportation Assessment Study (MTAS), published February 2026, presents a more conservative picture. The reference HALEU-moderated reactor has an overall starting TRL of 2–3, with key challenges being the development of 1200 K-suitable TRISO fuel forms and stable long-life hydride moderator materials. The SP-100-derived fast-spectrum reactor design starts at a slightly higher TRL of 3–4, benefiting from extensive reactor fuel testing performed during the SP-100 program. The MTAS power conversion and PMAD subsystems are projected at higher TRL but remain unvalidated at flight-relevant scale. This downward revision extends the feasibility timeline: achieving TRL 5 in the mid-to-late 2020s to support 2030s human mission design is now the stated NASA goal — not TRL 5 by 2026 as earlier studies assumed.

**SR-1 Freedom as validation milestone:** The Artemis V&V gate matrix must now center on SR-1 Freedom as the primary NEP heritage event. In early 2026, NASA Administrator Isaacman announced SR-1 Freedom — described as the first nuclear-powered interplanetary spacecraft — targeting launch in 2028. This system will demonstrate advanced NEP in deep space and deploy three Ingenuity-class helicopters at Mars before continuing into the outer solar system. Critically, DARPA canceled the competing DRACO nuclear thermal propulsion program in June 2025 after concluding that theoretical performance gains were less than anticipated, meaning SR-1 is now the sole near-term nuclear propulsion demonstration. The gap between SR-1 Freedom (20 kWe tech demo scale) and the ≥1 MWe required for crewed Mars transit remains a factor of ~50 in power — a sobering scaling challenge that must be addressed explicitly in mass budgets and programmatic timelines.

**SR-1 Freedom power constraint:** At 20 kWe, SR-1 Freedom delivers $F \approx 0.49$ N. At $m = 100$ t, $a \approx 4.9 \times 10^{-6}$ m/s² → approximately 39 years to achieve 6 km/s. This is instructive: SR-1 is a technology demonstration, not a crew-viable propulsion system. It validates reactor operation, radiator deployment, and NEP system integration heritage — essential precursors — but scaling to ≥1 MWe requires at minimum one additional development cycle post-2028.

### 3.3 MARVL Modular Radiator Architecture

A previously unmodeled mass-reduction pathway deserves inclusion in the propulsion trilemma. NASA Langley's MARVL (Modular Assembled Radiators for Nuclear Electric Propulsion Vehicles) program divides the NEP heat dissipation system into smaller components that can be assembled robotically and autonomously in space. This modular approach directly attacks the radiator mass term, which has been the dominant contributor to system-level specific mass $\alpha$. If MARVL achieves design goals, the $\alpha$ range of 5–10 t/MWe could be revised downward to 4–7 t/MWe for a 2 MWe system, reducing total NEP system mass from 18–35 t to approximately 14–28 t. This should be carried as a sensitivity parameter in all mass budget calculations:

**Baseline (no MARVL):** $m_{\text{NEP}} \approx 18\text{–}35$ t at 2 MWe

**With MARVL:** $m_{\text{NEP}} \approx 14\text{–}28$ t at 2 MWe (best-case radiator mass reduction ~25%)

The trilemma inequality remains: $t \propto 1/\sqrt{P/m}$, $D_{\text{rad}} \propto t$, $m_{\text{launch}} \propto \alpha P$. MARVL shifts the feasible boundary but does not eliminate it. A 1 MWe flight-qualified system ($\sim$8–15 t total with MARVL) remains the minimum threshold that fits within Starship-class lift margins if ISRU payload is offloaded to surface deployment.

