## 5. ISRU Stoichiometry, Catalysis, Site Dependency & Dust Tolerance

### 5.1 Sabatier-RWGS Hybrid Pathway

Baseline Sabatier: $\text{CO}_2 + 4\text{H}_2 \to \text{CH}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O}$. If subsurface water is scarce, reverse water-gas shift (RWGS) reduces H₂ demand:

$$\text{CO}_2 + \text{H}_2 \xrightarrow[\text{400°C}]{\text{Cu/ZnO/Al}_2\text{O}_3} \text{CO} + \text{H}_2\text{O}$$

Equilibrium favors CO₂ at low $T$; catalyst loading and thermal management are critical. Followed by methanation: $\text{CO} + 3\text{H}_2 \to \text{CH}_4 + \text{H}_2\text{O}$. Net H₂ requirement drops ~50%.

### 5.2 MOXIE-Anchored Scaling Target

**Revision:** The ISRU oxygen production target of 3 kg/hr requires grounding in validated empirical data. The MOXIE experiment on Perseverance — the first ISRU demonstration on another planet — achieved a peak production rate of 12 g/hr of oxygen at 98% purity or better, exceeding NASA's original 6 g/hr design goal. Over its operational lifetime, MOXIE generated a total of 122 g of oxygen. Critically, a future ISRU system would need to be scaled up approximately 200 times to autonomously provide at least 24 metric tons of liquid oxygen for ascent vehicle propellant in the 16 months preceding crew launch. This 200× scaling factor is the empirical basis for the 3 kg/hr design point (12 g/hr × 200× / 800 operating hours ≈ 3 kg/hr continuous). All ISRU architecture trades must reference this scaling ratio explicitly, not treat 3 kg/hr as an arbitrary specification.

Separately, a 5× scaled solid oxide electrolysis (SOE) stack tested by OxEon Energy and NASA/JPL demonstrated 2–3 kg/hr oxygen production at Mars pressure — providing direct hardware validation of the intermediate scaling step. This result should be cited as TRL evidence for SOE feasibility at sub-mission scale.

### 5.3 Power-Mass Coupling

SOE enthalpy: $\Delta H = 9$ MJ/kg O₂, system efficiency $\eta \approx 0.70$. For scaled ISRU target $\dot{m}_{O_2} = 3$ kg/hr:

$$P_{\text{elec}} = \frac{3 \times 9.0}{0.70} \approx 10.7 \text{ kWe}$$

A 1–2 MWe NEP reactor can dedicate $<1\%$ capacity to continuous ISRU. Feasibility is site-specific: polar ice deposits enable closed-loop H₂O recycling; equatorial sites require RWGS with CO byproduct management or imported H₂.

### 5.4 Dust Tolerance as a Hard ISRU Failure Mode

**New constraint — dust contamination of gas intake systems:** The Sabatier-RWGS-SOE pathway depends critically on atmospheric CO₂ intake through filtration systems. Martian dust presents a failure mode not previously quantified in the stoichiometric model. Martian dust's electrostatic, magnetic, and fine-grained qualities make contamination of habitat systems and equipment inevitable — directly analogous to Apollo lunar dust ingress, but chemically more hazardous. Martian dust storms in 2018 and 2022 caused the loss of the Opportunity Rover and InSight Lander after dust accumulation prevented sufficient power generation — both cases of common-cause failure driven by a single environmental agent.

For ISRU specifically, dust accumulation on SOE gas intake filters reduces effective atmospheric flow, degrading oxygen production efficiency. A dust-tolerance efficiency degradation term $\epsilon_d(t)$ must be added to the ISRU stoichiometric model:

$$\dot{m}_{O_2}^{\text{effective}} = \dot{m}_{O_2}^{\text{nominal}} \cdot \epsilon_d(t), \quad \epsilon_d(t) = e^{-\lambda_d t}$$

Where $\lambda_d$ is the dust fouling rate constant, which is currently unmeasured on Mars at human mission timescales. Technologies developed to limit dust contamination include habitat air filters, electrostatic repulsion devices, and self-cleaning functions — but the dust's electrostatic and fine-grained properties mean contamination remains probable regardless. CLPS precursor missions must measure $\lambda_d$ in situ before ISRU system sizing is finalized.

Every Martian year (686.98 Earth days), Mars experiences regional dust storms coinciding with southern summer. Every approximately three Martian years (5.5 Earth years), planet-encircling global storms occur. A 600-day surface stay will encounter at minimum one major regional storm and carries a non-trivial probability of encountering a global storm. ISRU power and throughput budgets must include a storm-season degradation scenario with $\epsilon_d \approx 0.3$–$0.5$ during peak opacity events, validated against Curiosity's documented visibility decreases during the 2018 global storm.

### 5.5 Regolith Sintering Chemistry

Martian regolith ($\sim$45% SiO₂, 18% FeO, 7% Al₂O₃) vitrifies at $T > 1000$°C to ferrosilite glass ($\rho \approx 2.5$ g/cm³). Microwave or resistive sintering yields structural bricks with dual utility: habitat construction and radiation shielding. A 2 m wall provides 500 g/cm² areal density, reducing GCR flux to $<10\%$ of unshielded levels. Energy requirement: $\sim$1.2 MJ/kg. For 100 t habitat shielding: $E \approx 33$ MWh, feasible with NEP idle power over 3–4 days.

**Dust toxicity constraint on sintering operations:** Martian regolith contains toxic elements including silica, iron oxides, and perchlorates. High-temperature sintering operations may generate finer aerosolized particulates with increased respirable fraction. Robotic sintering operations should be conducted exclusively by automated systems with crew isolated inside habitat during active sintering phases. This constraint adds an operational sequencing requirement to habitat construction timelines.

