## 6. Latency, Autonomy & Information-Theoretic Bounds

Earth-Mars one-way communication delay: $T \in [240, 1440]$ s. Closed-loop terrestrial control bandwidth is Nyquist-limited:

$$f_c < \frac{1}{2 T_{\text{delay}}} \Rightarrow f_c < 0.0004 \text{ Hz for } T = 1200 \text{ s}$$

Autonomy requirement is task-dependent and modeled as a first-order response to delay:

$$A(T) = 1 - e^{-T/\tau}$$

Human decision latency $\tau$ varies by operation: landing maneuvers $\tau \approx 2$ s, EVA anomaly response $\tau \approx 30$ s, habitat systems management $\tau \approx 120$ s. For $T = 1200$ s, $A \geq 0.99$ for all critical tasks. NASA's Perseverance 200+ m AI-planned traverses validate $A \approx 1.0$ operational readiness for surface navigation.

The constraint shifts to **trust calibration**. Optimal delegation threshold:

$$\text{Delegate if } p_{\text{AI}} > p_{\text{human}} + \frac{c_{\text{switch}}}{\Delta U}$$

Where $c_{\text{switch}}$ is cognitive switching cost and $\Delta U$ is utility difference. For life-critical decisions ($\Delta U \to \infty$), delegation requires $p_{\text{AI}} \approx 1.0$.

