## 7. Actuarial Risk, REID, Fracture Risk & Ethical Feasibility

### 7.1 Radiation REID Budget

NASA STD-3001 Vol 1 Rev B (2022) specifies age- and gender-averaged REID coefficient $k \approx 3.1$–$6.2 \times 10^{-5}$ mSv⁻¹ (45-yr-old baseline). Linear no-threshold (LNT) model: $\text{REID} = k \cdot D$.

**Solar-max feasible window with BNNT-LC:** $D_{\text{total}} \approx 122$ mSv (transit + EVA, updated for BNNT-LC attenuation). $\text{REID} \approx 0.38$–$0.76\%$. Monte Carlo 95% CI: [0.22%, 1.45%]. Expected crew loss for $n = 4$:

$$E[L] = n \cdot \text{REID} \approx 4 \times 0.0057 = 0.023 \text{ deaths/mission}$$

**Ethical threshold:** No agency will approve missions with $E[L] > 0.05$ (1 death per 20 missions). The solar-max/BNNT-LC window satisfies this constraint with modest margin.

**LNT caveat and DREF:** LNT may overpredict chronic low-dose GCR risk. If NASA adopts a Dose-Rate Effectiveness Factor (DREF) = 2 for spaceflight GCR, the effective transit dose drops to ~30 mSv-equivalent, pushing REID to ~0.09–0.18% and expanding the feasible window significantly. Mechanistic DNA repair models — calibrated against NASA CIPHER longitudinal data — must replace LNT before final crew certification. This is not merely a recommendation but a mission-critical research priority: the difference between DREF=1 and DREF=2 determines whether a 4-person crew carries a 0.023 or 0.011 expected fatality — a distinction that affects institutional approval thresholds.

### 7.2 Musculoskeletal Fracture Risk as a Parallel Actuarial Constraint

**New actuarial line item:** Published modeling (Stavnichuk et al., *npj Microgravity*, 2020) using femoral neck BMD data from 69 astronauts predicts that 62% of opposition-class mission astronauts and 100% of conjunction-class mission astronauts will develop osteopenia during mission, with 33% at risk for osteoporosis. A mission-incapacitating fracture during EVA or surface operations represents an actuarial risk separate from REID and must be budgeted independently.

The fracture risk constraint is modeled as:

$$P(\text{fracture}) = P(\text{BMD} < \text{threshold}) \times P(\text{loading event during EVA})$$

For a 600-day surface stay with current countermeasures (aRED + bisphosphonates + vitamin D/calcium): $P(\text{BMD} < \text{threshold}) \approx 0.33$ for worst-case crew. This represents an unacceptably high probability of mission-incapacitating injury for any individual crew member, particularly given 20-minute surgical response limitations on the Martian surface. The fracture risk constraint effectively mandates: (1) pre-mission selection of crew with above-average bone density reserves; (2) Mars-specific exercise countermeasure validation at Artemis III before crew certification; and (3) pharmaceutical countermeasure trials at partial gravity levels, not just microgravity.

