## 13. Conclusion

Human Mars exploration in 2026 is a solvable engineering problem — but only within a narrowly defined, quantitatively bounded feasible envelope that is simultaneously tighter and better-characterized than prior analyses suggested. This updated envelope requires:

**(1) Solar-maximum trajectory alignment** to reduce GCR exposure by ≥30% — non-negotiable regardless of shielding advances.

**(2) BNNT liquid crystal film shielding at ≥20 g/cm²** with buildup-corrected storm refuges. The 2025 KIST/KAIST liquid crystal breakthrough — achieving 3.7× neutron shielding improvement and 15% superior mass efficiency over aluminum, validated in NASA joint simulation — upgrades this constraint from aspirational to near-ready. Spacesuit BNNT-LC integration could extend EVA dose margins by an additional 10–15%.

**(3) NEP propulsion at ≥1 MWe**, enabled by MARVL modular radiators to reduce specific mass $\alpha$. The MTAS TRL 2–3 starting point for HALEU-based systems and the 50× power gap between SR-1 Freedom and crew-mission requirements make this the longest-lead constraint in the entire program. SR-1 Freedom's 2028 launch is essential but insufficient — a dedicated 100 kWe–1 MWe scaling demonstration must follow before 2033.

**(4) ISRU power coupling with active dust tolerance management**, anchored to MOXIE's validated 200× scaling target of 24 MT LOX and parameterized by in-situ dust fouling rate measurements still outstanding from precursor missions.

**(5) Autonomy levels $A \geq 0.99$** for all task-critical responses, with SPE forecasting upgraded to 72-hour warning capability via PINNBARDS and white-box XAI systems achieving TSS ≥ 0.80.

**(6) System reliability $R_{\text{sys}} \geq 0.91$**, achievable only with active dust-sealed redundancy architecture — the Mars dust erosion amplifier (1.5× reliability reduction, 5× surface roughness increase) makes unmitigated designs catastrophically unreliable at $R_{\text{sys}} \approx 0.31$.

**(7) Musculoskeletal fracture risk below 5% per crew member**, requiring Artemis III partial-gravity physiology validation, CIPHER-calibrated bone modeling, and Mars-specific pharmaceutical countermeasure trials before crew certification.

**(8) Dust respiratory health management**, with HEPA and electrostatic air systems sized for continuous 600-day operation against perchlorate, silica, and iron oxide aerosols — a ~150–200 kg habitat mass addition not previously budgeted.

The Artemis lunar program remains the essential validation matrix for these constraints, but with a critical schedule caveat: Artemis is running behind the timeline assumed in prior Mars architecture studies. Every Artemis surface milestone that slips past 2030 either opens an unvalidated medical risk or forces mission delay to the 2043–2046 solar-max window. Agencies must recognize that the Artemis schedule is not a parallel program — it is on the critical path of every crewed Mars feasibility gate.

The feasible envelope is explicit, testable, and actionable. The constraint budget is the blueprint. The programmatic clock is running.

